  "final_text": "I've created the dashboard and the alert rule. The rule is enabled and Kibana reports its status as `ok`.\n\n**What the data holds:** about 14,000 web requests, timestamped from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03. Each request records the client IP, URL, response code, bytes served, file extension, host, operating system and source/destination country. Only three response codes appear: 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801) and 503 (441).\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-bd3939fd Web logs overview\"** ([open it](http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-bd3939fd-web-logs-overview-c76772d33882)) has 7 panels:\n- Single-number panels for total requests, unique visitors (counted by client IP) and total bytes served.\n- Requests over time, as bars split by response code.\n- A pie chart of the response-code breakdown.\n- A table of the top 10 URLs, with request count and average bytes.\n- A bar chart of the top 10 destination countries.\n\nThe data runs nearly two months past today, so the dashboard's default time range runs from 7 days ago to 60 days ahead.\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-bd3939fd 5xx spike\"** (id `23022498-c96e-46ac-9635-cb61f2e37dcc`)\n- It runs every minute against `kibana_sample_data_logs`, using the `timestamp` field.\n- It fires when more than 10 requests with a 5xx code arrive within 5 minutes.\n- It matches 5xx codes as response values from 500 up to (but not including) 600. I ran that filter against the index and it returned all 441 of the 503s.\n- No notification is attached, so it will flag alerts in Kibana but won't send them anywhere.",
